* Layered Operators or operators or operands in shared namespaces and all layered products installed through OLM:
** Use a limited default deny policy: If the pods can coexist with other pods owned by another controller or end-user in a shared namespace, set it to match a label on your pods: [+Default Deny for Egress and Ingress for Layered Products+|https://docs.google.com/document/d/1CDoGSRd-h8VT4PMrK_83Ro0YzYPjORbkxtfTJU1sN6Q/edit?tab=t.0#heading=h.87rv8zjn31mc].Use a default deny policy
** The operator manifest must contain a network policy for the operator pods
** The operator must create policies for all operand pods it creates


h3. Default Deny for Egress and Ingress for Layered Products

However, layered products that get installed into arbitrary namespaces, or the openshift-operators namespace, should not deny all pod traffic because they may break traffic from other pods in the namespace that they do not control.  Instead, a policy should be applied to all pods with a matching label.  It probably makes sense to have a common label for all pods created by your operator, if you do so, then you can apply a policy that selects that but denies all ingress and egress traffic.

 

Note: It is far better to have a wildcard that matches the whole namespace in case a label gets missed, or some attacker manages to spin up a new pod.  So, only use this if necessary.

 

